Vendor-validate uncertain behaviour in already-implemented builtins.

Some builtins ship with implementations that satisfy the documented
contract under the spec's *headline* description, but make
implementation choices for edge cases the spec doesn't pin down.
We need to test those choices against real AutoCAD and BricsCAD to
either confirm or correct them.

Convention (project-wide, going forward):

  * Every builtin whose behaviour rests on an unverified assumption
    carries an inline `;;; SPEC-UNCERTAIN:` marker in its CL
    docstring / comment block. One marker per uncertain point.
    Greppable from the source with:

        grep -nE "SPEC-UNCERTAIN" \
             clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/*.lisp

  * Every marker has a matching entry in this issue (below) so
    we can plan a focused research pass instead of rediscovering
    each uncertainty one builtin at a time.

  * Once vendor-validated, the marker is replaced by a concrete
    `;;; Vendor: ...` cite (filename:line of the matching
    `autolisp-spec` Tested Behaviour section, or a probe-script
    name), and the issue entry is moved to the resolved tail.

Open uncertainties (open this list as you add SPEC-UNCERTAIN
markers — keep it sorted by function name):

== >= (comparison-operators-vendor-semantics) ==

  * BricsCAD rejects (>= 'a nil) with a type error where AutoCAD
    returns T (and BricsCAD itself returns T for (> 'a nil)). Under
    --dialect bricscad clautolisp reproduces the error for ANY left
    argument that is neither nil, a number nor a string. Probed only
    with a symbol: does BricsCAD also reject (>= '(1) nil) and
    (>= T nil)? And does it reject (<= nil 'a), the mirror case?

== TRANS (M2) ==

  * Behaviour when the source space (FROM) is not equal to the
    target space (TO) in a *loaded* drawing. Our headless engine
    has no UCS, no viewport DCS, no PSDCS — every space
    collapses to WCS, so identity is the only correct answer
    here. But Autodesk's TRANS on a fresh empty drawing where
    UCS == WCS — does it still apply some normalization (e.g.
    z-fill, rounding) we'd need to mirror? Probe needed.

  * What happens with a 3D point when TO is DCS or PSDCS (which
    are 2D coordinate spaces)? Does TRANS drop Z, project, or
    return a 3-tuple with z=0? Currently we return (x y z) with
    z preserved.

  * The DISPLACEMENT-P fourth argument: when true, the point is
    a free vector (rotation only, no translation). Our identity
    transform handles points and vectors the same way (no-op),
    so the flag has no observable effect. Once we have a real
    UCS/DCS matrix, DISPLACEMENT-P must skip the translation
    component. Locking in the flag's documented contract via a
    probe is what's needed here.

  * FROM / TO as entity-name lists (ECS, Entity Coordinate
    System). We accept the argument shape but ignore the entity
    identity. What does AutoCAD return for
    (trans pt 0 (entlast)) on a 2D entity's ECS?

== TEXTBOX (M2) ==

  * Width calculation. We approximate width as
    `0.6 × height × character-count` — a monospace-font ratio.
    Real AutoCAD looks the width up per-character in the active
    text style's SHX or TTF font. The 0.6 ratio is a placeholder
    until we ship a font-metrics back-end. What ratio does
    Autodesk use as a fallback when no font is loaded? Is it
    fixed-per-style, or computed?

  * Lower-left corner. We return ((0 0 0) (w h 0)) — lower-left
    at the origin. Real TEXTBOX returns the box in the entity's
    OCS centered/aligned per the text's justification (40/41/72/
    73 DXF group codes). We currently ignore justification.
    Probe: what does Autodesk return for a centered-text
    entity?

  * Behaviour when the entity-list lacks DXF group 1 (text
    string) or group 40 (height). Currently we default to ""
    and 1.0 — but Autodesk may raise an error instead. Probe.

== MEM (M2) ==

  * Triple layout: Autodesk's docs say "approximate values" but
    don't lock the slot order or units. We assume
    (used free reserved) in bytes — but BricsCAD V26 might use
    (used reserved free) or units of kilobytes. Probe needed.

  * Static / stack accounting. CCL's room output includes Stacks
    and Static rows; we only consume the Lisp Heap row. Should
    they fold into one of the three slots? Currently neither
    appears in our triple.

== Entity COM properties and the block walk (vla-entity-property-bridge) ==

Carried over when vla-entity-property-bridge was closed (2026-09-26).
ANSWERED 2026-10-03 by probes/sources/probe-block-walk.lsp (suites
block-walk + text-metrics; AutoCAD 2022 accoreconsole and BricsCAD V26
macOS, runs committed under probe-results/). Two of the four answers
showed clautolisp WRONG; fixed in clautolisp 2.2.154 (MR !376).

  * entmakex on a BLOCK / ENDBLK pair. BOTH vendors accept it. BLOCK
    returns an ENAME (the block header, eq to tblobjname), each member its
    ENAME; ENDBLK returns nil on AutoCAD, the block NAME (a string) on
    BricsCAD. The block is defined. clautolisp refused BLOCK/ENDBLK under
    entmakex -- FIXED (ENDBLK follows BricsCAD under a BricsCAD dialect).

  * The entnext walk from (tblobjname "BLOCK" name) yields the members
    ONLY -- no ENDBLK, on both. clautolisp already agreed (now tested).

  * Name / EffectiveName. For a plain (non-dynamic) INSERT, BricsCAD
    gives Name = EffectiveName = the block name, as clautolisp does.
    AutoCAD's accoreconsole has no ActiveX, so it cannot answer. STILL
    OPEN: a DYNAMIC block's reference (*U<n> vs EffectiveName) -- entmake
    cannot create one, so it needs a probe that loads a drawing carrying a
    dynamic block on BricsCAD (or full AutoCAD). Low value: clautolisp has
    no dynamic blocks to represent.

  * Text metrics. A vendor's TEXTBOX is the glyph INK box (left bearing
    included), with no kerning; AutoCAD and BricsCAD agree on every
    horizontal value. Vertically AutoCAD reports an off-baseline box as
    touching the baseline and gives spaces no ink; BricsCAD reports the
    true ink and counts spaces to their advance. BricsCAD's TEXT
    GetBoundingBox equals TEXTBOX at the origin. clautolisp's 0.6 x height
    ratio and zero-width TEXT box were wrong -- FIXED with the measured
    per-glyph table (autolisp-runtime/source/text-metrics.lisp, make -C
    clautolisp text-metrics). clautolisp's probe output now equals
    AutoCAD's on 206/210 records (the rest: no ActiveX on AutoCAD) and,
    under --dialect bricscad-mac, BricsCAD's on 209/210 (a random block
    name). Not measured: other text styles / fonts, non-ASCII glyphs
    (clautolisp measures them as "n").

== QNEWTEMPLATE — does AutoCAD document it? (pjb to confirm) ==

Opened 2026-09-26 while giving clautolisp a template for new drawings
(dwg-round-trip-loses-entities). pjb: "il doit y avoir une sysvar pour
specifier un template non?" — and he is right that a sysvar is the
mechanism. The question is the vendor NAME.

  * From memory, AutoCAD's knob is QNEWTEMPLATE (string, the template
    file the QNEW command uses, set in Options). It is in NEITHER the
    specification NOR system-variables-inventory.sexp — and that
    inventory was GENERATED from help.autodesk.com 2026 ENU, so its
    absence is evidence, not an oversight of ours. Three readings:
    (a) the enumeration missed it; (b) AutoCAD 2026 no longer exposes it
    as a sysvar; (c) it never was one and Options is the only surface.
    Probe: (getvar "QNEWTEMPLATE") on AutoCAD 2026, and grep the 2026
    system-variable reference.

  * BricsCAD's TEMPLATEPATH *is* in the spec, but it is the Templates
    FOLDER, not a file — so it cannot be the whole answer on either
    vendor. clautolisp honours it for resolving a relative name.

  * What clautolisp does meanwhile: CLAUTOLISPNEWDRAWINGTEMPLATE, a
    CLAL-namespace extension sysvar (the documented exception for
    surface with no vendor counterpart), default "" = the empty document
    of every previous release. If the probe confirms QNEWTEMPLATE,
    accepting it as a second spelling is two lines here, plus a spec
    entry (Compatibility: AutoCAD only) and the mandatory
    document-version bump — pjb's call, since it changes the normative
    spec.

== IntersectWith (ActiveX entity method, clautolisp 2.3.10) ==

  Source: clautolisp/cador/source/intersect.lisp (SPEC-UNCERTAIN in its
  header). Probe: autolisp-front-end/tests/scenarios/entities/
  intersectwith-probe.lsp, jobs intersectwith:probe:*. Full list in
  issues/open/cador-intersectwith-missing.issue (TO MEASURE):

  * the ORDER of the returned points (cador: along the base object);
  * how far an open polyline extends (cador: first span backwards, last
    span forwards; a closed one not at all), and a RAY under
    acExtendThisEntity;
  * tangency (one point), overlapping lines / coincident circles (no
    point), a crossing at a polyline vertex (once), plan crossings at
    different Z (no point);
  * vlax-safearray->list of the EMPTY result (cador: nil).

== Future entries ==

When opening a new SPEC-UNCERTAIN marker, add an `== NAME (Mn) ==`
section here and list the specific questions. Group questions per
function; keep the same wording style (one paragraph per
uncertainty).

== Resolved ==

  * < <= > >= on non-nil incompatible arguments (opened 2.2.88):
    resolved 2026-09-25 by the comparison-operators probe on AutoCAD
    2022 and BricsCAD V25/V26 -- both signal a type error for a number
    against a string, a symbol or a list; nil is the smallest value, not
    bottom. See issues/closed/comparison-operators-vendor-semantics.issue.
